---
title: Reopen a period
description: When and how a completed quarter or financial year is reopened, and what reopening does.
sidebar:
  order: 14
---

A completed quarter is locked: entries dated in it cannot be posted. To change it, a manager reopens it.

## Before reopening

Consider first whether the change can be recorded without reopening:

- An item dated in the locked quarter can often be recorded in the next open period with **Record in next open period…**.
- A later payment can still be applied to a bill dated in a locked quarter.

## Reopen a quarter

{% steps %}

1. Open the completed quarter's case and select **Reopen quarter**.
2. Give the **Reason for reopening**.
3. Confirm.

{% /steps %}

{% screenshot id="bookkeeping/reopen-quarter-dialog" caption="Reopening a quarter requires a reason." /%}

## What reopening does

- The quarter is unlocked for further entries, and its F5 returns to unconfirmed.
- No posted entry is changed or deleted. Corrections are made by reversing and entering again.
- Entries dated in the reopened quarter are marked as changes after close.
- The reopening, its reason and the person who made it are recorded in the review list.
- When the case is already complete, a new quarterly case is created for the same company and quarter; the completed case stays complete and links to it. Complete the quarter again when the changes are made.

## Financial years

If the financial year has been closed, reopen the year first under **Year end › Year close**, then reopen the quarter.
